{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>But we are guilty concerning our brother.</b> This is the way of the righteous: when they sin, they confess the matter and justify the judgment upon themselves, as it is written (Leviticus 5:19): ״אשם הוא אשם אשם לה׳״. And the way of the wicked is that they sin and say, 'We have not sinned'; this is what is written (Zechariah 11:5): ״אשר קוניהן יהרגון ולא יאשמו״." "<b>That we saw the distress of his soul when he pleaded with us.</b> This indicates the view of those who hold that the brothers never sold him, for if so, they should have said, 'But we are guilty that we sold our brother.' Rather, the guilt was that they cast him into the pit, for there is no doubt that he pleaded with them not to do this, and they did not listen to him." "<b>Therefore this has come upon us.</b> Its correct meaning is: upon the truth and upon the judgment has this trouble come upon us — in the language of (Numbers 27:7): ״כן בנות צלפחד דוברות״. And so they justified the judgment upon themselves, for just as they did not take pity on him when they saw the distress of his soul, this trouble came upon them — measure for measure."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 42, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:19:56.159715830Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}